WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.312 --> 00:00:02.167
- He's an American author and business

00:00:02.167 --> 00:00:04.231
skills training consultant.

00:00:04.231 --> 00:00:06.389
Ink Magazine named him the world's

00:00:06.389 --> 00:00:08.827
number one small business guru.

00:00:08.827 --> 00:00:11.777
He's the author of the mega
bestseller, The E Myth.

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:13.250
He's Michael Gerber, and here are

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.667
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:20.461
- It's the most extraordinary
businesses in the world

00:00:20.461 --> 00:00:23.260
are focused and finding a better way.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.181
They're what I call best way companies.

00:00:25.181 --> 00:00:27.340
They're constantly trying
to find a better way.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.267
To do what, to do what they do.

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:30.583
To do what, to differentiate

00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:32.187
themselves from everybody else.

00:00:32.187 --> 00:00:35.275
How, by giving the customer
the sense of control

00:00:35.275 --> 00:00:37.850
in our business that they
don't have any place else.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.850
The cleaning workings of Disneyland.

00:00:41.702 --> 00:00:42.982
You know, you ask people,
what do you remember

00:00:42.982 --> 00:00:47.149
about the park and what
they all say is it's clean.

00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:49.954
Now think about it, they
don't talk about Mickey,

00:00:49.954 --> 00:00:51.549
they don't talk about Pluto,
they don't talk about.

00:00:51.549 --> 00:00:54.442
They say it's clean, now why in the world

00:00:54.442 --> 00:00:56.210
would that matter to people?

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.883
Because every place else is so dirty!

00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:02.235
How did Walt do that?

00:01:02.235 --> 00:01:06.402
Every night at Disneyland, they
steam clean the entire park.

00:01:07.527 --> 00:01:10.785
And they've been doin' that
in Anaheim for over 35 years.

00:01:10.785 --> 00:01:14.952
Think about that, to do what,
to create this sense of order

00:01:17.782 --> 00:01:19.856
in the mind of the consumer.

00:01:19.856 --> 00:01:22.047
Which instantly differentiates
them from everybody else.

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:24.400
Every place else is
dirty, our place is not.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.035
The core business development system

00:01:27.035 --> 00:01:29.909
in any company, in every
company, sitting here,

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:31.933
no matter what kind of company it is,

00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:34.560
is lead generation, lead
conversion, client fulfillment.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.258
How many people do you
attract to your door?

00:01:36.258 --> 00:01:38.615
How do you convert a larger
and larger percentage

00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:40.874
of those people to become customers?

00:01:40.874 --> 00:01:42.886
And how do you convert
the largest percentage

00:01:42.886 --> 00:01:45.906
of those customers to become clients?

00:01:45.906 --> 00:01:49.526
And the ballgame of that is mindset,

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:52.668
it's truly the paradigm through which

00:01:52.668 --> 00:01:56.553
you approach the building of the systems

00:01:56.553 --> 00:01:58.840
through which leadership
and entrepreneurship

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.788
becomes a reality every
single day in the company.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.969
Most important thing you
can learn about being an

00:02:04.969 --> 00:02:09.900
entrepreneur is a blank piece
of paper and beginner's mind.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.233
Everything begins right now, everything.

00:02:14.607 --> 00:02:18.774
Now is the most important word
you can begin to practice.

00:02:19.658 --> 00:02:22.658
Now, not before, not later, but now.

00:02:24.048 --> 00:02:27.965
And being present, now,
is the art of mastering

00:02:29.257 --> 00:02:31.924
your human capability right now,

00:02:32.958 --> 00:02:35.321
like a martial artist on the mat.

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.488
Right now, be here, now, and begin it.

00:02:39.729 --> 00:02:42.979
The system is the solution, not people.

00:02:44.064 --> 00:02:47.972
Not the product, the
system is the solution.

00:02:47.972 --> 00:02:51.889
Because Ray Kroc and Tom
Watson and Walt Disney

00:02:53.083 --> 00:02:54.587
and the founder of Midas Muffler and

00:02:54.587 --> 00:02:57.418
the founders of Supercuts,
every single one of 'em knew

00:02:57.418 --> 00:03:00.240
identically the same thing, the one thing

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.457
the consumer wants more
than anything else,

00:03:02.457 --> 00:03:06.297
the one product that is true
of every great business,

00:03:06.297 --> 00:03:10.069
that every entrepreneur is there
to create in that business,

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:12.444
the one word that describes what you're

00:03:12.444 --> 00:03:15.316
selling in your business, is control.

00:03:15.316 --> 00:03:20.247
C-O-N-T-R-O-L, the consumer's control over

00:03:20.247 --> 00:03:24.738
their experience when
they come to buy from you.

00:03:24.738 --> 00:03:26.193
And if you can give that customer

00:03:26.193 --> 00:03:29.044
what they want the very first time,

00:03:29.044 --> 00:03:31.354
and you can replicate it faithfully,

00:03:31.354 --> 00:03:34.322
every single time from that point on,

00:03:34.322 --> 00:03:37.213
that customer will continually
choose to come back.

00:03:37.213 --> 00:03:41.192
And that's the business Ray Kroc was in.

00:03:41.192 --> 00:03:42.700
And he realized that if it was up to

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.105
his franchisee, he'd be out of business.

00:03:46.105 --> 00:03:48.355
Because the franchisee,
being a technician,

00:03:48.355 --> 00:03:49.900
would go and do it, do it, do it, do it,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.485
and destroy it in the process.

00:03:51.485 --> 00:03:52.912
Ray Kroc realized that McDonald's

00:03:52.912 --> 00:03:55.123
wasn't in the restaurant business.

00:03:55.123 --> 00:03:58.227
It was in the fuel business.

00:03:58.227 --> 00:04:00.933
Fill me up, you got it,
fill me up, you got it.

00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:02.116
And he did it every single time

00:04:02.116 --> 00:04:04.533
and identically the same way.

00:04:06.635 --> 00:04:09.892
And it was his ability to
do it every single time

00:04:09.892 --> 00:04:14.769
in identically the same
way, in a clean, organized,

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:19.212
shining, colorful environment
that differentiated

00:04:19.212 --> 00:04:22.630
McDonald's from every
other restaurant around.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:25.482
I want to do something
I've never done before,

00:04:25.482 --> 00:04:28.875
and I don't know how,
but I can imagine it.

00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:30.924
I want to engage in the unknown.

00:04:30.924 --> 00:04:34.260
I truthfully want to pursue the unknown.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.253
And in the pursuit of the unknown,

00:04:36.253 --> 00:04:38.824
something comes to realization.

00:04:38.824 --> 00:04:40.978
And I'm never prepared for it.

00:04:40.978 --> 00:04:44.783
So when I set out to do
what I set out to do,

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:46.652
I set out to do the impossible.

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:47.702
Everybody told me, "You're nuts.

00:04:47.702 --> 00:04:49.506
"You can't do that. You can't do that."

00:04:49.506 --> 00:04:53.310
And that's also consistent
with all entrepreneurs.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.136
There's always a legion of
people who will tell them,

00:04:57.136 --> 00:04:58.724
"You can't do that, and even if

00:04:58.724 --> 00:05:00.953
"you could, why would you?

00:05:00.953 --> 00:05:04.369
"There's so many better
things to do other than that,

00:05:04.369 --> 00:05:06.886
"that you can make so
much more money from."

00:05:06.886 --> 00:05:11.247
But money, was really a
very, very narrow part

00:05:11.247 --> 00:05:13.099
of what I was really setting out to do.

00:05:13.099 --> 00:05:15.945
Course I had to make a living,
course I had to do this,

00:05:15.945 --> 00:05:20.357
course I wanted to, you know,
produce something great,

00:05:20.357 --> 00:05:23.191
but it was really, the result that

00:05:23.191 --> 00:05:26.173
I could intuit, that in fact, that

00:05:26.173 --> 00:05:29.750
people who were were
struggling, don't have to.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.480
That they're struggling so hard,

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.423
is simply a sign that something's
missing in this picture,

00:05:36.423 --> 00:05:38.474
and so the question always has been,

00:05:38.474 --> 00:05:43.106
and I believe this to be the
true entrepreneurial driver,

00:05:43.106 --> 00:05:44.818
what's missing in this picture?

00:05:44.818 --> 00:05:46.653
What's missing in this picture?

00:05:46.653 --> 00:05:49.327
It's always a question,
it's never an answer.

00:05:49.327 --> 00:05:51.180
What's missing in this picture?

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.450
And as I begin to ask that question,

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:57.530
something begins to come
alive, to give me the food

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:01.697
I need to nurture this passion
I've got that I can't apply.

00:06:03.597 --> 00:06:07.594
And it enables me to see
things I never saw before.

00:06:07.594 --> 00:06:10.473
- Get the first one right,
you get your sales system

00:06:10.473 --> 00:06:13.983
right when you're small, you get your

00:06:13.983 --> 00:06:16.965
lead generation system
right when you're small

00:06:16.965 --> 00:06:19.389
and you get your lead
conversion system right when

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:22.781
you're small, knowing that
you're going to be big.

00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:25.644
And that because you're going to be big,

00:06:25.644 --> 00:06:28.725
it's absolutely critical
that you've gotten it right

00:06:28.725 --> 00:06:32.190
so you can replicate it
tens of thousands of times.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.319
The attorney opens up a legal practice.

00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:37.581
The doctor opens up a medical practice.

00:06:37.581 --> 00:06:40.499
The graphics designer opens
up a graphics design business.

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:42.316
Each and every one of
them believing because

00:06:42.316 --> 00:06:45.081
I understand how to do the
technical work of a business,

00:06:45.081 --> 00:06:46.864
I understand how to run a business

00:06:46.864 --> 00:06:48.442
that does that technical work.

00:06:48.442 --> 00:06:50.795
And it's 180 degrees from the truth.

00:06:50.795 --> 00:06:53.022
Knowing how to do the work in a business

00:06:53.022 --> 00:06:57.189
has nothing to do with
creating a business that works.

00:06:58.223 --> 00:06:59.968
And it is the fatal assumption behind

00:06:59.968 --> 00:07:04.135
the failure of almost every
single business you walk into.

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:07.708
Every single business around.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.841
Because the owner of that business,

00:07:10.841 --> 00:07:12.926
the founder of that business, does not

00:07:12.926 --> 00:07:16.681
start a business as an entrepreneur would.

00:07:16.681 --> 00:07:18.347
The founder of that
business starts a business

00:07:18.347 --> 00:07:20.570
for absolutely the wrong reason.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:24.278
They start a business
to get rid of the boss.

00:07:24.278 --> 00:07:26.631
They start a business
to get rid of the boss

00:07:26.631 --> 00:07:28.599
because they're workin' for somebody else,

00:07:28.599 --> 00:07:29.690
they're doin' it, doin' it, doin' it,

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:32.276
day in and day out, a master technician,

00:07:32.276 --> 00:07:35.506
a master auto mechanic,
a master poodle clipper,

00:07:35.506 --> 00:07:37.323
and they're sittin' there
sayin' to themselves,

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:38.997
"Why am I doin' this for this guy?

00:07:38.997 --> 00:07:41.720
"I could be doin' this for me."

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.340
"Hell, any dummy can run a business,"

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.515
you said, "I'm workin' for one!"

00:07:45.515 --> 00:07:47.651
(audience chuckles)

00:07:47.651 --> 00:07:50.019
And knowing that, you decide,

00:07:50.019 --> 00:07:53.451
"I could do this as well as this guy."

00:07:53.451 --> 00:07:55.261
And you start a business,

00:07:55.261 --> 00:07:58.669
to get rid of the boss and instantly do

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:02.502
what you never should
have done, create a job.

00:08:03.752 --> 00:08:05.295
So what I'm going to suggest,
ladies and gentlemen,

00:08:05.295 --> 00:08:08.355
for the purpose of this
meeting, that most businesses

00:08:08.355 --> 00:08:10.402
you walk into aren't businesses at all.

00:08:10.402 --> 00:08:13.903
They're a job for the
person who started it.

00:08:13.903 --> 00:08:17.034
And he's created the worst job of all.

00:08:17.034 --> 00:08:19.810
Workin' 12 hours a day, seven days a week,

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.795
doin' it, doin' it, doin' it, doin' it,

00:08:21.795 --> 00:08:25.875
based upon one presupposition
that sweat equity

00:08:25.875 --> 00:08:28.767
is what every little business is about.

00:08:28.767 --> 00:08:31.063
Who came up with that word?

00:08:31.063 --> 00:08:33.024
But a technician, who's accustomed to

00:08:33.024 --> 00:08:34.937
working it, working it,
working it, working it,

00:08:34.937 --> 00:08:36.834
doing it, doing it, doing it, doing it,

00:08:36.834 --> 00:08:38.319
hasn't the faintest idea of how

00:08:38.319 --> 00:08:40.092
to create a business that works,

00:08:40.092 --> 00:08:43.496
simply creates a job that
depends upon himself or herself,

00:08:43.496 --> 00:08:46.000
and continues to do it over
and over and over and over

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.395
and over again until they
don't want to do it anymore.

00:08:50.395 --> 00:08:54.518
Most businesses imprison
the person who creates it.

00:08:54.518 --> 00:08:57.657
Most important thing
I can say to you about

00:08:57.657 --> 00:09:01.500
growing and about creating
and about learning

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:05.095
how to bring the entrepreneur
within you to life

00:09:05.095 --> 00:09:07.648
and to manifest your ideas in the world,

00:09:07.648 --> 00:09:10.823
is to truly become an incredible student.

00:09:10.823 --> 00:09:14.168
You truly have to learn how to develop

00:09:14.168 --> 00:09:18.335
the capability and capacity
to create in the world.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:20.834
In order to do that, there're really five

00:09:20.834 --> 00:09:23.381
essential skills you need to develop.

00:09:23.381 --> 00:09:26.166
The first is this skill of concentration.

00:09:26.166 --> 00:09:28.898
You need to be able to
focus your attention.

00:09:28.898 --> 00:09:31.385
The second is discrimination.

00:09:31.385 --> 00:09:34.271
You need to be able to
choose what's most important,

00:09:34.271 --> 00:09:36.804
as opposed what's least important.

00:09:36.804 --> 00:09:38.885
The third is organization.

00:09:38.885 --> 00:09:41.169
Your really have to turn chaos into order

00:09:41.169 --> 00:09:44.310
and you can find a way to
do that, where you live,

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.279
in your school, at home, at work,

00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:49.779
with your peers, and so forth.

00:09:50.891 --> 00:09:52.862
The fourth skill is innovation.

00:09:52.862 --> 00:09:54.871
That's the best way, in other words,

00:09:54.871 --> 00:09:56.915
it's trying to discover a better way

00:09:56.915 --> 00:09:59.212
to do everything that needs to be done,

00:09:59.212 --> 00:10:00.418
and you can learn how to do that

00:10:00.418 --> 00:10:02.672
in everything you're doing today.

00:10:02.672 --> 00:10:07.579
So you have the opportunity
to innovate all around you,

00:10:07.579 --> 00:10:09.672
with everyone you know, and to begin

00:10:09.672 --> 00:10:12.361
to practice innovation as a way of life.

00:10:12.361 --> 00:10:16.739
And finally, and absolutely
essential, is communication.

00:10:16.739 --> 00:10:19.771
You have to learn how to
connect with other people.

00:10:19.771 --> 00:10:23.339
You can't simply sit
all alone, in your home.

00:10:23.339 --> 00:10:26.192
You can't simply use Facebook and Twitter.

00:10:26.192 --> 00:10:29.979
You got to be able to
communicate live, in person,

00:10:29.979 --> 00:10:31.685
and learn how to do that, and you learn

00:10:31.685 --> 00:10:35.240
how to do that by
practicing how to do that.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:38.528
So find a great communicator
and say to that individual,

00:10:38.528 --> 00:10:40.389
"How did you learn how to communicate?"

00:10:40.389 --> 00:10:42.090
And that person will, in fact,

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.782
begin to teach you how to communicate.

00:10:44.782 --> 00:10:47.537
So concentration,
discrimination, organization,

00:10:47.537 --> 00:10:51.156
innovation, communication,
and you're on your way.

00:10:51.156 --> 00:10:54.371
It's like a voice awakens inside of you.

00:10:54.371 --> 00:10:56.768
That's what I call the
entrepreneur within,

00:10:56.768 --> 00:10:59.688
which is the subject of my latest book.

00:10:59.688 --> 00:11:03.586
It awakens, it either
awakens or it doesn't.

00:11:03.586 --> 00:11:07.614
And if does, what most of
us do is put it to sleep.

00:11:07.614 --> 00:11:09.420
Because it's dangerous, I mean essentially

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.350
it's saying fly against all of the odds,

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.391
do something that you
don't know how to do,

00:11:15.391 --> 00:11:18.675
create something that
you've never created before,

00:11:18.675 --> 00:11:21.854
pursue a path that's impossible to pursue,

00:11:21.854 --> 00:11:26.021
look at how many fail, look
at how few ever make it, and

00:11:27.548 --> 00:11:28.824
then what?

00:11:28.824 --> 00:11:30.907
Well, that called me, and

00:11:32.574 --> 00:11:35.283
I've always been called by the impossible.

00:11:35.283 --> 00:11:38.697
So, I never worked for anybody else.

00:11:38.697 --> 00:11:41.649
I was never on the job so to speak.

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:44.740
The only job I was really
on, my very first job,

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.434
was when I was going to school,

00:11:46.434 --> 00:11:50.466
and I worked in a Ford
plant, and I was a janitor.

00:11:50.466 --> 00:11:52.957
And I was working at night, obviously

00:11:52.957 --> 00:11:55.054
to support myself going to school,

00:11:55.054 --> 00:11:57.534
and I was changing a light
bulb, and I'll never forget it,

00:11:57.534 --> 00:11:59.939
the boss came up to me and he said,

00:11:59.939 --> 00:12:02.383
"You don't look too happy, Gerber."

00:12:02.383 --> 00:12:04.320
And I said, "I didn't know
I was supposed to be."

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:05.604
(host laughs)

00:12:05.604 --> 00:12:08.842
And he said, "No, you
have to be happy here.

00:12:08.842 --> 00:12:12.401
"That's part of the game we play."

00:12:12.401 --> 00:12:15.637
And I said, "Yeah, but I'm doin' the job."

00:12:15.637 --> 00:12:18.072
And he said, "Not good enough.

00:12:18.072 --> 00:12:21.082
"Unless you're happy, you got to be gone."

00:12:21.082 --> 00:12:22.881
And that was my last night there.

00:12:22.881 --> 00:12:24.885
And I never had a job since.

00:12:24.885 --> 00:12:26.638
I've always worked for myself.

00:12:26.638 --> 00:12:29.505
"I am my business," every one says.

00:12:29.505 --> 00:12:30.616
"It it weren't for me, there wouldn't

00:12:30.616 --> 00:12:33.434
"be a business," they say proudly.

00:12:33.434 --> 00:12:36.000
But what happens when you
don't want to do it anymore?

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.680
You're out of business.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.948
And I want to talk about a man who

00:12:40.948 --> 00:12:44.215
I'm sure most people are familiar with,

00:12:44.215 --> 00:12:46.033
at least the company he created,

00:12:46.033 --> 00:12:47.020
most people are familiar with,

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:50.520
and that's Tom Watson, the founder of IBM.

00:12:51.564 --> 00:12:54.372
You know, when I talk about
IBM to small business people,

00:12:54.372 --> 00:12:55.592
they think to themselves, "Well,

00:12:55.592 --> 00:12:57.588
"what is he talkin' about IBM for?

00:12:57.588 --> 00:13:01.788
"I mean, you know, I'm not
IBM, I don't want to be IBM."

00:13:01.788 --> 00:13:03.719
But just think about it for the moment.

00:13:03.719 --> 00:13:04.868
I think you got to agree with me

00:13:04.868 --> 00:13:08.594
IBM is a relatively successful company.

00:13:08.594 --> 00:13:12.011
You know, last year, IBM did $59 billion.

00:13:14.421 --> 00:13:17.235
Last year, do you know $59, do you

00:13:17.235 --> 00:13:18.648
know what a billion dollars is?

00:13:18.648 --> 00:13:20.355
A billion dollars, ladies and gentlemen,

00:13:20.355 --> 00:13:23.022
is $1,000 a day, for 3,000 years

00:13:24.649 --> 00:13:26.604
in small, unmarked bills.

00:13:26.604 --> 00:13:29.708
(audience laughs)
Think about that.

00:13:29.708 --> 00:13:31.708
59 times that last year.

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:35.956
I mean, can you see it?

00:13:35.956 --> 00:13:37.739
How did you do that, Tom?

00:13:37.739 --> 00:13:40.723
I mean, you got to wonder,
what did the guy know

00:13:40.723 --> 00:13:44.617
that most people, IBM is
bigger than most countries.

00:13:44.617 --> 00:13:47.787
(audience chuckles)

00:13:47.787 --> 00:13:51.763
IBM is, if not the, one
of the most profitable

00:13:51.763 --> 00:13:55.930
businesses in the entire world,
in the history of mankind.

00:13:57.195 --> 00:14:00.528
Nobody has created anything to match it.

00:14:02.236 --> 00:14:06.625
Well you got to ask yourself,
what did the guy know?

00:14:06.625 --> 00:14:09.346
Well somebody asked him the question.

00:14:09.346 --> 00:14:13.168
And Tom Watson was purported to respond

00:14:13.168 --> 00:14:15.186
in the following way, he said,

00:14:15.186 --> 00:14:18.625
"I did something in IBM
that most people don't do.

00:14:18.625 --> 00:14:19.792
"Three things.

00:14:20.872 --> 00:14:23.157
"The critical three things," he said,

00:14:23.157 --> 00:14:25.467
"that differentiate IBM
from everybody else."

00:14:25.467 --> 00:14:27.273
He said, "The first
thing is I had a picture

00:14:27.273 --> 00:14:30.065
"of what IBM would look like
when it was finally done

00:14:30.065 --> 00:14:31.857
"before I even started the business.

00:14:31.857 --> 00:14:33.638
"I had a vision of what the business

00:14:33.638 --> 00:14:36.520
"would look like when it was finished."

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:38.596
You know, we talk about
visionaries, what is a visionary?

00:14:38.596 --> 00:14:40.531
A visionary is someone
who can see something,

00:14:40.531 --> 00:14:43.290
where out there, no in his head.

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:46.931
He had a vision in his head, a picture

00:14:46.931 --> 00:14:49.137
of how the business would
look when it was finally done.

00:14:49.137 --> 00:14:51.348
He saw this extraordinary organization,

00:14:51.348 --> 00:14:53.989
this extraordinary company, this monument,

00:14:53.989 --> 00:14:57.149
this testament to order, to control,

00:14:57.149 --> 00:14:59.292
to discipline, to integrity, the most

00:14:59.292 --> 00:15:01.478
extraordinary service
business in the world,

00:15:01.478 --> 00:15:05.061
is what he imagined he
was about to create.

00:15:06.274 --> 00:15:07.735
"So once I had a picture
of what the business

00:15:07.735 --> 00:15:10.081
"needed to look like, when it
was finally done," he said,

00:15:10.081 --> 00:15:12.182
"The second thing I did, was to get

00:15:12.182 --> 00:15:15.384
"a picture of how our people would look.

00:15:15.384 --> 00:15:18.530
"The people that would
be in IBM to produce

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.016
"that vision that I had in my mind."

00:15:21.016 --> 00:15:24.166
And he said, "And I saw this
extraordinary army of people,

00:15:24.166 --> 00:15:26.363
"in their dark suits and
their white, crisp shirts,

00:15:26.363 --> 00:15:29.030
"and their black shining shoes."

00:15:30.086 --> 00:15:33.829
Missionaries, his army of missionaries

00:15:33.829 --> 00:15:35.612
out there in the world
communicating to the world

00:15:35.612 --> 00:15:37.856
in a way that nobody had every
communicated to it before.

00:15:37.856 --> 00:15:41.837
And he said, "That was
the person, the IBM man,"

00:15:41.837 --> 00:15:44.837
he called it then, excuse me ladies.

00:15:46.059 --> 00:15:47.083
He said, "Finally, the third thing

00:15:47.083 --> 00:15:50.097
"we did at IBM that
nobody else did," he said,

00:15:50.097 --> 00:15:52.069
"Once I had a picture of what
the business would look like

00:15:52.069 --> 00:15:54.164
when it was finally
done, once I had picture

00:15:54.164 --> 00:15:55.708
of how our people needed to look

00:15:55.708 --> 00:15:58.312
in order to bring that vision
to the rest of the world,"

00:15:58.312 --> 00:16:01.222
he said, "the third
thing I realized is that

00:16:01.222 --> 00:16:02.894
"there is absolutely no difference

00:16:02.894 --> 00:16:06.644
"between a big business
and a small business.

00:16:07.903 --> 00:16:09.843
"A big business is
simply a small business,"

00:16:09.843 --> 00:16:12.843
Tom Watson said, "that
did the right things.

00:16:12.843 --> 00:16:14.668
"A big business is a small business

00:16:14.668 --> 00:16:16.099
"that did the right things."

00:16:16.099 --> 00:16:17.894
He said, "If we were to start our business

00:16:17.894 --> 00:16:20.334
"in the right way, and do it as though

00:16:20.334 --> 00:16:24.746
"it were the biggest
business in the world,

00:16:24.746 --> 00:16:27.673
"if we're able to act in the beginning

00:16:27.673 --> 00:16:31.590
"as though this business
were already complete,

00:16:32.892 --> 00:16:35.345
"only then would we be able to do that."

00:16:35.345 --> 00:16:37.845
So he said, "We didn't go to work in IBM,

00:16:37.845 --> 00:16:40.437
"we went to work on IBM to replicate

00:16:40.437 --> 00:16:43.520
"the picture I had in my mind."

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:44.751
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:16:44.751 --> 00:16:48.017
I made this video because
Mike Watkins asked me to,

00:16:48.017 --> 00:16:49.336
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:16:49.336 --> 00:16:51.418
that you want me to profile next,

00:16:51.418 --> 00:16:53.988
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:53.988 --> 00:16:55.973
I'd also love to know
which of Michael Gerber's

00:16:55.973 --> 00:16:58.831
top 10 rules meant the most to you.

00:16:58.831 --> 00:17:01.443
Leave it in the comments and
I will join in the discussion.

00:17:01.443 --> 00:17:02.651
Thank you so much for watching.

00:17:02.651 --> 00:17:06.234
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

